THERE ARE 4 TYPES OF LINKED LIST PROBLEMS ON LEETCODE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

TYPE 1 — FAST & SLOW POINTERS → cycle, middle, palindrome
TYPE 2 — REVERSAL → reverse full, partial, in k-groups
TYPE 3 — TWO POINTER / MERGE → merge sorted, nth from end, intersection
TYPE 4 — NODE REARRANGEMENT → reorder, partition, split and rejoin

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
TYPE 1 — FAST & SLOW POINTERS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Detect if a cycle exists
→ Find the start of a cycle
→ Find the middle of a list
→ Check if a list is a palindrome

The idea: slow moves 1 step, fast moves 2 steps.
If there is a cycle, fast will eventually lap slow and they meet.
If no cycle, fast reaches null first.
When fast reaches the end → slow is at the middle.

TEMPLATE:

ListNode* slow = head;
ListNode* fast = head;

while (fast && fast->next) {
    slow = slow->next;
    fast = fast->next->next;
    if (slow == fast) {
        // cycle detected
    }
}
// no cycle — slow is now at the middle
EXAMPLE — Linked List Cycle
https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle

bool hasCycle(ListNode* head) {
    ListNode* slow = head, *fast = head;
    while (fast && fast->next) {
        slow = slow->next;
        fast = fast->next->next;
        if (slow == fast) return true;
    }
    return false;
}
EXAMPLE — Linked List Cycle II (find start of cycle)
https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle-ii

After fast and slow meet inside the cycle:
reset slow to head, keep fast where it is.
Move both one step at a time.
Where they meet again = the start of the cycle.

WHY THIS WORKS:
Let a = distance from head to cycle start.
Let b = distance from cycle start to meeting point.
Slow traveled a+b. Fast traveled 2(a+b) = a+b + full cycle.
So a = full cycle - b = distance from meeting point back to start.
Moving one pointer from head and one from meeting point → they meet at start.

ListNode* detectCycle(ListNode* head) {
    ListNode* slow = head, *fast = head;
    while (fast && fast->next) {
        slow = slow->next;
        fast = fast->next->next;
        if (slow == fast) {
            slow = head;
            while (slow != fast) {
                slow = slow->next;
                fast = fast->next;
            }
            return slow;
        }
    }
    return nullptr;
}
EXAMPLE — Middle of the Linked List
https://leetcode.com/problems/middle-of-the-linked-list

When fast reaches the end, slow is at the middle.
For even-length lists this returns the second middle node.

ListNode* middleNode(ListNode* head) {
    ListNode* slow = head, *fast = head;
    while (fast && fast->next) {
        slow = slow->next;
        fast = fast->next->next;
    }
    return slow;
}
EXAMPLE — Palindrome Linked List
https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-linked-list

Step 1: Find middle using fast/slow.
Step 2: Reverse the second half.
Step 3: Compare first half and reversed second half value by value.

