COMMON MISTAKES AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

MISTAKE 1 — Not using a dummy node
Special-casing the head for deletions or insertions creates messy code.
Just use a dummy node — it makes all positions uniform.

MISTAKE 2 — Losing the next pointer before reversing
In reversal: ALWAYS save ListNode* next = cur->next
BEFORE setting cur->next = prev.
Forgetting this loses the rest of the list permanently.

MISTAKE 3 — Not cutting the list after finding the middle
When splitting into two halves (e.g. Reorder List, Palindrome):
set slow->next = nullptr to terminate the first half.
Without this, the first half still links into the second half.

MISTAKE 4 — Off-by-one in remove nth from end
Move fast n+1 steps (not n) when using a dummy node as the start.
This places slow one node before the target, so slow->next is the node to delete.

MISTAKE 5 — Forgetting to set the tail's next to nullptr
After rearranging or partitioning, the last node in a chain may still
point to something from the old list. Always set the tail's next = nullptr.

